@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print JSON text with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The JSON is parsed and re-serialized with indentation (4 spaces by default), and highlighted using the console theme. Set ``indent`` to change the number of spaces, or ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output may be produced by logging a JSON renderable, which is available as :class:`rich.json.JSON`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'))
+
+The package also provides a ``print_json`` function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[1, 2, 3]')
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, but under default printing long lines are cropped to the console width. Enable soft wrapping (``soft_wrap=True``) to keep full lines.
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line, using the ``rich.json`` module::
+
+    $ python -m rich.json foo.json
+
